indexing;
In C++, indexing is zero-based, meaning the first element of a collection is always at index 0, and the last element is at index size - 1. Indexing allows you to read or modify elements within arrays, strings, and standard library containers like std :: vector.
It also allows us to access different parts of arrays, strings ,vectors  even in classes.

Slicing;
In C++, "slicing" usually refers to object slicing. When a derived class object is assigned to a base class object in C++, the derived class object's extra attributes are sliced off (not considered) to generate the base class object; and this whole process is termed object slicing. In simple words, when extra components of a derived class are sliced or not used and the priority is given to the base class's object this is termed object slicing. 
In C++, a derived class object can be assigned to a base class object, but the other way is not possible. To tackle this slicing problem we can use a dynamic pointer.
Moreover, object slicing happens when a derived class object is assigned to a base class object, and additional attributes of a derived class object are sliced off to form the base class object.


Reshaping;

Unlike languages like Python (with NumPy), C++ does not have a built-in reshape function for standard arrays or vectors.
How you achieve reshaping in C++ depends on whether you want to rearrange elements into a new 2D structure, create a 2D view over a 1D container, or use a dedicated linear algebra library.
1. Manual Reshaping (The Standard Approach)
If you have a 1D vector (or a flattened 2D vector) and want to physically redistribute its elements into a new 2D grid structure (std::vector<std::vector<T>>), you can use simple index arithmetic.
2.The Modern C++ Way: (std::mdspan)
If you do not want to allocate new memory or copy data, you can create a multidimensional view over existing flat contiguous memory using std::mdspan. This mimics NumPy's behavior of returning a view instead of a copy
I chose the method 2 since it was easy to understand

